                  -----------------------------------------                <  ATerm : A terminal program for OS9/68000 >                  -----------------------------------------                                Introduction                              ================   Here is a communication program for the OS9/68000 user !!!   This long awaited type of program, has finally reached your computer !   Not being an expert in the 68000 assembly language, it was rather dif-ficult to me, at the first glance, to know how I would make it work.  Thisprogram was originaly written by a friend of mine.  He wrote it a littlewhile ago for a 68000 experimental board.  When he got his ATARI, he thentransported it to OS9/68000.   He ended by writing the XModem (CheckSum) routines, but had to stop theupgrade of the program as he was taking classes.  So I took the opportunityto learn more about the 68000 assembly under OSK by continuing the upgrade.   I must say that this being my first experience using the 68000 assemblylanguage and I'm quite surprised how well the things turned out.   Enough talking, let's go for some real stuff !  Here we are ...  I pre-sent you, ATerm !!   This program has been written in a such way to be as user friendly aspossible.  It is 'menu driven', has easy prompts to follow and the overallconception will facilitate the future expansions.   Here are the main options available in the ATerm program :   1)  Baud rate selection : 50 - 19200 : (Tested: 4800 Upl, 9600 Down.)   2)  CR/LF Translation   : Will substitute any received CR for a CR/LF.   3)  Download ASCII      : Download ASCII to disk file.                             ( Remotely controlled buffer is supported )   4)  Echo                : Used when contacting a half duplex host   5)  Flow Control        : User defined handshake characters. (Xon/Xoff)   6)  Phone Dialer        : Dial, set baud and parity for up to 35 sites   7)  Echo to printer     : Duplicate received characters to printer/file   8)  Shell Access        : Invoke a Shell within ATerm. (ESC to return)   9)  Upload ASCII        : Prompted / Delayed ASCII text files upload                             ( Control of the remote's buffer supported )  10)  XModem              : XModem UpLoad/DownLoad. ( CheckSum / CRC )  11)  YModem              : YModem 1K ( 1024 bytes packet ) *   *  DownLoad 'batch' mode under developpement.   Plans are made in order to support Kermit, and CIS B+ file transfer pro-tocols.  We were first interested to release the actual version an see therelated comments.  Before going too far, we rather prefer having some feed-backs from the users, helping us to improve the actual version.   Hoping to hear from you, we now continue with a more detailed descrip-tion of ATerm, and how to use it.                                   Page  1                      -----------------------------------------                <  ATerm : A terminal program for OS9/68000 >                  -----------------------------------------                              How to use ATerm                              ================   ATerm has been designed to be easy to use.  It includes many options whichwill be described later.   ) How to invoke ATERM.     - ATerm is started at the system comand level, issuing :     ATerm {/T1}     - /T1 is the device descriptor name for the serial port.  ATerm will       check for an SCF device type having the same device driver name as       the one specified in the control file, otherwise you'll see :       ------------------------------------------------------------------            *** The specified device is not a valid one.  Sorry !            *** Must be a SCF type device having the right driver.       ------------------------------------------------------------------     - If no serial pathname is given, then the following intro message       will be shown on your screen.       ------------------------------------------------------------------                    ATerm : A terminal program for OS9/68000                    ===== = = ======== ======= === =========                       Syntax  : ATerm /serial_path                       Function: Terminal program for OSK                       Example : ATerm /t1       ------------------------------------------------------------------     - Upon a valid specification of the serial path, the main menu will       be displayed as ...     <===================================================================>     |                              ATerm Vx.x                           |      >-----------------------------------------------------------------<     |   B - Baud & Parity selections          C - CR/LF translation     |     |   D - Download ASCII text file          E - Echo Toggle           |     |   F - Flow characters selection         I - Invoke phone dialer   |     |   O - Close download file (ASCII)       P - Printer toggle        |     |   Q - Quit ATerm & Return to system     S - OSK Shell access      |     |   T - Terminal mode                     U - Upload ASCII file     |     |   X - XModem (128 Bytes CSum/Crc)       Y - YModem (1024 Bytes)   |      >-----------------------------------------------------------------<     |         [CTRL]-[Y] or [HELP] will re-display this screen.         |     <===================================================================>                                   Page  2                      -----------------------------------------                <  ATerm : A terminal program for OS9/68000 >                  -----------------------------------------                                Using  ATerm                              ================   First, it is important to understand that in order to access the diffe-rent ATerm's functions, you must press ^Y ( or the [HELP] key on the ATARIST keyboard ) then, at the "Make your selection : " prompt, press the keycorresponding to the desired option.  )) B - BAUD & PARITY SELECTIONS :     Choosing this option will bring you the 'menu' below.  Simply make up     your selection by pressing the key, number/letter, matching your rate.                              * Baud rate table *           0 = 50         1 = 75         2 = 110         3 = 134.5           4 = 150        5 = 300        6 = 600         7 = 1200           8 = 1800       9 = 2000       A = 2400        B = 3600           C = 4800       D = 7200       E = 9600        F = 19200       > Press the corresponding key, or {RETURN} to ignore request :     As you can see, the selection is quite straight forward.     Once the baud rate is set, you'll then be prompted to change the parity     setup, if necessary.  If your answer is positive, then three items will     be set upon your choice :       1)  PARITY      : None, Odd or Even     (Bits 0-1) -\       2)  DATA BITS   : 5, 6, 7 or 8          ("  " 2-3)   )-- *       3)  STOP BIT(S) : 1, 1.5 or 2           ("  " 4-5) -/      * Meaning of this information will be discussed in the "DIALER" section.     >> The default values are read from the option path descriptor.  )) C - CR/LF TRANSLATION :     This option will allow ATerm to translate all the incoming Carriage     Returns, to a Carriage Return / Line Feed sequence.  When receiving     text from the host/remote, if all the lines are double spaced, you'll     then have to disable this feature.     If the received text is printing all on the same line, you'll have to     enable the CR/LF translation option.     Note that even if you select this option, the extra line-feed generated     will not be echoed to the printer, if this one is active.                                   Page  3                        -----------------------------------------                <  ATerm : A terminal program for OS9/68000 >                  -----------------------------------------                                Using  ATerm                              ================  )) D - DOWNLOAD ASCII TEXT FILE :     This option will capture all the incoming data to the pathname speci-fied at the prompt "> Specify file name. ( [RETURN] = Cancel demand ) :".     If you simply press the [RETURN] key, this will void the request.  Ifyou specify a valid filename, you'll then be asked the following question.   "Do you want the automatic buffer feature ? [Y/N] :"  A positive answerto this question, will indicate to ATerm to alternatively Open / Close thebuffer upon the reception of the control characters, respectively, DC2/DC4which are in fact, ^R/^T.  Some stations uses this facility, but not all.   Make yourself sure that your selection is appropriate, otherwise you'llend up with ZERO byte of valuable information saved !!!   If the remote station uses this feature, then ATerm will issue a messagewhenever it receive the control character to toggle the buffer state.  )) E - ECHO TOGGLE :     This option enable ATerm to echo the characters back to your terminalwhen the host / remote is not echoing them to you.  When online, if you arenot able to see what you're typing, there is obviously a need to use thisfeature.  If you use it, but are not in the need of, the characters you'lltype will then appear twice.  In this case, just disable the feature.  )) F - FLOW CHARACTERS SELECTION :     Permit to the user to customize the characters that will serve to pau-se the remote, when you want to look at something on the screen for few mo-ments, or when ATerm is writing to disk, mainly during ASCII DownLoads.     By default, the XON is set to $11 (^Q), and XOFF is set to $13 (^S).So, when depressing the ^S key, this will stop the host from sending datato you and ^Q will resume action.     To specify the flow characters that you need, you'll have to enter theHEXADECIMAL value at the corresponding prompt.  As the actual definition ofXON/XOFF is widely used, we suggest you to try it as is before changing it.  )) I - INVOKE PHONE DIALER :     The phone dialer in this program, need you to have a certain knowled-ge on the data communication.  Still easy to use, but in order to set theproper parameters at the right place, you'll need to read the following in-formation, carefully !!                                   Page  4                         -----------------------------------------                <  ATerm : A terminal program for OS9/68000 >                  -----------------------------------------                                Using  ATerm                              ================  )) I - INVOKE PHONE DIALER : (Continued)     There are few things that need to be set before using this option.The file, containing the information, is a plain ASCII data file that canbe edited upon your need.  The format is the following :                   I] Host Identificat. : @BPM  #___-____                   | \___              /  |  \   \_________Identifier (1-9,A-Z)    |             /   |__ \____       |                        |            /      |     |       |                    Delim. #1  Delim. #2  Baud  Par.Mask  Phone Number Ind.     > Identifier : This will be the key that you'll have to press in order                    to select this entry via the menu given by ATerm.     > Delim. #1  : MUST be ']'.  This indicate to ATerm where the infor-                    mation start for a particular entry.  This is also on                    this character that ATerm estimates the quantity of #                    available in the file.     > Host Ident.: Comment that serves to identify the remote / host.  It                    is recommended to use a maximum of 19 characters for                    this field, as you'll be able to put two entries on the                    same line.  Using more, will force you to put each en-                    try on a separate line.     > Delim. #2  : MUST be ':'.  Terminate the Host Identification field.                    ATerm will use it in order to display the Host Id while                    dialing the number.     > Baud marker: Baud marker.  MUST be '@'.  Indicated to ATerm to take                    the next character as the baud rate selection.  See the                    'Baud Rate Table' to use the proper value.     > Parity     : This is a mask that will be used to set the modem parity.                    It is the summation of the values required for the Parity,                    Data Bits as well as the Stop Bit(s).                    DATA BITS   : 5-[12], 6-[08], 7-[04], 8-[00]  (Bits 2-3)                    PARITY      : None-[00], Odd-[01], Even-[03]  ("  " 0-1)                    STOP BIT(S) : 1-[00], 1.5-[16], 2-[32]        ("  " 4-5)                   * N.B. : Bits 6-7 are reserved for the system use.     As an exemple, for 7 Data Bits, Even Parity and 1 Stop Bit, this will     be, [04]+[03]+[00]=07. The PM field will be replaced by DECIMAL '07'.     *** Note here that the leading zero is NEEDED.              1] Host No. 1        :@A00  #555-1212 (2400 Bauds 8N1)              2] Host No. 2        :@707  #555-1213 (1200  " "  7E1)                                   Page  5                        -----------------------------------------                <  ATerm : A terminal program for OS9/68000 >                  -----------------------------------------                                Using  ATerm                              ================  )) I - INVOKE PHONE DIALER : (Continued)     > Phone No.  :  What is the purpose of a phone dialer without a phone                     number to dial !!!  The Phone Number MUST be lead by                     a '#' and have a trailing space or carriage return.                     In order to insure proper dialing operation, make your-                     self sure that you've also set the right 'modem prefix'                     and 'modem suffix' parameters in the control file.     Once you've set up your list of phone numbers, put it in the sub-direc-tory of /DD/SYS/ATERM using path name ATerm.Phone.  Once invoking the dialerfunction, the program will search the file as "/DD/SYS/ATERM/ATerm.Phone". *** You can use the environment variable PHONE to override this pathname.     ATerm is designed to support a maximum of 35 entries.  Having more thanthis quantity, will make it hard to read the list as it goes on the screen.     Having the limit at 35 and using a 2 entries per line format, will lookfine on your screen, therefore the selection will be easier to do too.  )) O - CLOSE DOWNLOAD FILE (ASCII) :     This option is self explanatory.  Its purpose is to close a previouslyopened download text file.  If you invoke it when there is no need, it isharmless.  If it is appropriate, the system will tell you how many byteswere written to the file and will give the corresponding pathname as well.     When switching from ASCII DownLoad to Upload, the system check for anyopened file, and close it if need is.  The same apply when going to XModemprotocol or exiting ATerm.  )) P - PRINTER TOGGLE :     When selecting this option, everything received from the modem will beshown on you screen as well as on your printer.  Your printer must have aninternal buffer in order to have this function operating properly.  At 300bauds the chances are good for most of the printers, but as soon as you use1200 baud and higher speed, your printer may not 'follow' the action.     Test it out and if it works, good for you.  Otherwise, use the DownLoadASCII option, and once the file is closed, you may print it as a backgroundtask, using the LIST command under the SHELL option of ATerm.  You may aswell print it when your session is terminated.     Sorry for this inconvenient, but there's no way to check the printerspeed and effectiveness via the program.  We'll think about it and may comeup with an alternative solution later on, unless you already know.  If so,please share it with us !!!                                   Page  6                        -----------------------------------------                <  ATerm : A terminal program for OS9/68000 >                  -----------------------------------------                                Using  ATerm                              ================  )) Q - QUIT ATERM & RETURN TO SYSTEM :     This is quite obvious, ain't it ?  When exiting ATerm to return to thesystem level, ATerm will check for any left opened files, and take the appro-priate action before giving you the control of your system.  )) S - OSK SHELL ACCESS :     Gives you the opportunity to access the SHELL and issue commands. This isusefull to exchange files between the RAM disk and another media, check forfree space and other facilities.  To return to ATerm, simply press the [ESC]key and you'll then be back to the ATerm's main menu.  )) T - TERMINAL MODE :      Simple and clear.  This option puts you online.  Whenever you're readyto communicate, after selecting the baud rate, opening the download file oranything else, you select this option to return back to the action !!  )) U - UPLOAD ASCII FILE :     Prompted and delayed ASCII uploads are available.  The 'prompted' sty-le, let you decide which character ATerm should 'see' before sending thefollowing line of text.  Also, a delay between the transmission of eachcharacter can be specified, in 1/100th of a second increment, ranging from0 to 9.  ( It should be noted that this is only an estimated delay. )     The 'delayed' style, uses a delay between the transmission of each linein 1/10th second increment, ranging from 1 to 9, as well as the delay bet-ween each characters.  This method can be more reliable on slow hosts.     It is imperative to remember that the file to be sent is a plain textfile, which don't contain control characters that may cause ATerm or thehost to hang.     If the need is, you may abort the UpLoad at any time, by pressing the[ESC] key.  Also, it is possible to have ATerm to control the 'remote cap-ture buffer'.  If you ask for the automatic buffer feature, ATerm will senda (^R / DC2) to open remote's buffer and a (^T / DC4) upon completion.     You must be awared if the host supports this feature before using it.No harm will be done, but undesirable characters will be inserted in thehost's buffer if the function is enabled, but not effectively supported.                                   Page  7                        -----------------------------------------                <  ATerm : A terminal program for OS9/68000 >                  -----------------------------------------                                Using  ATerm                              ================  )) X - XMODEM (128 BYTES CSUM/CRC) :     Allow you to exchange binary and text file using the XModem protocol.Both the checksum (8 bits) and the CRC (16 bits) validation methods arefully supported.     Using XModem, implies that the packets (data blocks), have 128 bytes.  )) Y - YMODEM (1024 BYTES) :     File transfer using the XModem protocol.  The main difference betweenYModem and XModem, is the fact that YModem is strictly using the CRC vali-dation method and the packet size is fixed to 1024 bytes.     Nevertheless, be 100% SURE to use the right protocol, otherwise theprogram may hang for unexpected reasons.     It looks like the ATerm's YModem implementation is compatible with mostof the host I've contacted so far.  Still, some of them, are using a diffe-rent 'ending' method, for which I've not found the appropriate documentation.     Delphi is one of them.  I'll have to contact the persons in charge andget the required information in order to make ATerm able to get the thingsright.                                   Page  8                        -----------------------------------------                <  ATerm : A terminal program for OS9/68000 >                  -----------------------------------------                                Control File                              ================   As to give more flexibility to the program, a control file has been se-lected to achieve this goal.  This file will located in the same directoryas the ATerm.Phone file, i.e /DD/SYS/ATERM.  The program will try to locatethe file using "/DD/SYS/ATERM/ATerm.Ctl" as path name.   At the moment being, the file MUST have 20 items defined in it, prior tostart the execution of ATerm.  Still, only the first 11 are really in use fornow, leaving some place for further expansion.   A FREE format have been elected, which will give you the opportunity toadd comments into it, without affecting the functionnality of ATerm.   ATerm is scanning the control file sequentially, and when it 'sees' a '@' or a '$', it then assume the following data to be a control code sequence.   So, in order to keep track of the quantity of the defined items, ATerm isincrementing a control counter for each '@' or '$' found.  Avoid to use thesecharacter within comments, otherwise this may lead to unexpected results.   I must emphase on the fact that the sequences have to be defined in thefollowing order. 'Clear Screen', 'Curson ON', 'Cursor OFF', 'Reverse ON','Reverse OFF', 'Printer initialisation string', 'Modem Prefix', 'Modem Suffix'and finally, the 'Device Driver', which is the device driver name of the pathused to communicate with the serial port.   Note here that you can put line comments between definitions. As long asyou don't have less than 20 'control sequences' defined, even if a less quan-tity is effectively used, ATerm will assume that they are all OK.  If you have strange results, take a close look at your control file.  Typoerrors is the most common error.   A maximum of 32 bytes is allowed per sequence definition.  Each hexadeci-mal value have to have its leading zero.  In order to facilitate the creationand reading of the control file, two different formats are supported.   Each sequence definition is preceded by a sequence marker, '@' or '$'.Then, you have an HEXADECIMAL VALUE representing the physical length of theresulting sequence string.  This value is in the range $00-$20.   The general format to define a code sequence is : TLLCCCCCC....CCCC.   T  : Indicate the format type of the code sequence.        - $ :  Sequence is specified using hexadecimal values.        - @ :  Representation of the sequence is in plain ASCII text.   LL : Length of the sequence, specified in HEXADECIMAL, with leading '0'.   C  :  - An HEXADECIMAL representation of a byte within the sequence, if           the '$' type is used.  Must have its leading '0' if necessary.         - An ASCII character, to be used without conversion, providing the           '@' format is in use for this sequence.                                   Page  9                  -----------------------------------------                <  ATerm : A terminal program for OS9/68000 >                  -----------------------------------------                                Control File                              ================   If the terminal you're using does not have a sequence equivalent to si-mulate or support the function, simply put a '00' as the physical length.   Doing so, this will simply disable the function.   Remember here that only the quantity specified by the length indicator,will be processed.  Any remaining data will be disgarded.   Also, keep in mind that NO MORE THAN ONE code sequence definition can beused ON THE SAME LINE.  ATerm is USING ONLY the FIRST definition it 'sees'on the line, ignoring the remaining text on the line.   Here is an example of what ATerm.Ctl may look like :-----------------------------------------------------------------------------                          <<<  ATerm Control File  >>>   Descriptions     Sequence code       Informations related to the codes ================  ===============  ========================================= Clear Screen      $021B45          For Vt-52 Video Cursor On         $021B65          For Vt-52 Video Cursor Off        $021B66          For Vt-52 Video Reverse On        $021B70          For Vt-52 Video Reverse Off       $021B71          For Vt-52 Video Printer Init.     $021B15          (CR=CR) : For TANDY DMP-105 printer Modem prefix      @04ATDT          "ATDT" Command Prefix.  (Hayes Compatible) Modem suffix      $010D            "[CR]"  "   "  Suffix.    " "    "    " Device Driver     @05stsio         Device driver identification. (Ser. Path) Pipe Buffer Size  $0400004000      Internal representation of $4000 (32 Bits) Printer Processor @10/R0/ATerm.Print&  Name of the script file to Print Dummy             $00              Not used for now "   "             $00               "  "  "  "   " "   "             $00               "  "  "  "   " "   "             $00               "  "  "  "   " "   "             $00               "  "  "  "   " "   "             $00               "  "  "  "   " "   "             $00               "  "  "  "   " "   "             $00               "  "  "  "   " "   "             $00               "  "  "  "   " ================  ===============  =========================================-----------------------------------------------------------------------------   As a little reminder, ATerm uses path name "/DD/SYS/ATERM/ATerm.Ctl" to gain access to this file.  Failing to have installed the control file underthis path name, will force ATerm to return to the system level, and notifyingthe user of the unsuccessfull access to the required file, thus requiring anappropriate installation.                                   Page  10                  -----------------------------------------                <  ATerm : A terminal program for OS9/68000 >                  -----------------------------------------                               Special  Notes                              ================   -  ATerm has been tested for baud rates up to and including 4800 with      successfull results for uploads.   -  XModem Downloads worked properly for baud rates up to 19600, using      a direct connection with a NULL MODEM.   -  As I don't have at hand documentation for the YModem Batch protocol,      so its installation has been delayed.  Is there a 'standard' as far      as the information contained in the first Block #0 is concerned ?   -  Path names "/DD/SYS/ATERM/ATerm.Ctl" and "/DD/SYS/ATERM/ATerm.Phone"      are to be considered as being the DEFAULT path names.  If the user      wants to use different files, he may set the environment variables      CNTLF and PHONE to reflect his needs.   -  The SHELL parameter may be used to in order to identify the Shell that      will be 'forked' via the option 'S' under ATerm.   -  Improvements have been done to accomodate hardcopy printout.  Now, the      environment variable HDCPY is used to specify the way to print incoming      information.      1) By default, ATerm is using direct 'line-edited' print to path /P,         if HDCPY is not defined.      2) SetEnv HDCPY Direct_/P2 : Direct output to /P2. (Same as HDCPY /P2).         > Only the 'D' is meaningfull in order to specify a direct mode.         > It is IMPERATIVE to have the printer path name embedded in the           word indicating the direct mode.  "D/P2" is sufficient, but using           "Direct_/P2" is more significant.      3) SetEnv HDCPY Script : Tells ATerm to use a script file to print.         > As well as the Direct mode, only the first letter is significant,           so, as long as the first letter of the HDCPY variable is a 'S',           ATerm will select this mode.         > The script file to be used is /DD/SYS/ATERM/ATerm.Print, unless           the pathname is overriden by the name specified in the control file.         > When using the 'script file' mode, ATerm opens a 'named pipe' in           order to send the data, in 'raw' mode.  The pathname used by the           program is '/Pipe/ATerm'.  (See supplied 'ATerm.Print' file.)         > Note that if you use a filter before sending text to the printer,           the filtering range must be specific in order to avoid to strip           control sequence code for a printer related function.         > The buffer size to be used by the 'pipe' MUST be defined in the           control file.  Otherwise, the default of 96 bytes is used !!!                                   Page  11                        -----------------------------------------                <  ATerm : A terminal program for OS9/68000 >                  -----------------------------------------                                 Conclusion                              ================   -  Hoping you'll enjoy using ATerm as much as I did to learn and improve      myself with the assembly language on the 68000.  I spent many hours at      debugging and installing new routines to ATerm.  Having good tools to      work with, is a must in order to appreciate what can be done.            ANY FEEDBACK WILL BE MORE THAN GREATLY APPRECIATED !!!            Programmers          GEnie        Delphi    Compuserve          ================   ============   =========  ============          Daniel Boulanger   D.BOULANGER1   SMARTCOCO   76650,1037          Denis  Chartrand       ----       USERDENIS   72561,2714          ================   ============   =========  ============                                   Page  12      